const e={id:"boss-1",part:1,title:"The World Builder's Trial",subtitle:"Prove your mastery of Part 1: Creating a World",description:"This assessment tests your ability to apply the neuroscience of storytelling. Each scenario requires you to synthesize multiple concepts from Chapters 1-8.",passingScore:80,timeEstimate:"15-20 min",questionsCount:12,xpReward:500,badgeId:"part1-world-builder",isBoss:!0,sections:[{id:"boss-intro",type:"content",title:"Welcome to the World Builder's Trial",content:[{type:"text",value:"You've learned how stories capture attention, how the brain builds reality, how worlds and characters come alive, and how meaningful change drives narrative. Now it's time to **put it all together**."},{type:"callout",variant:"key-concept",value:"This isn't a memory test. Each question presents a **storytelling scenario** that requires you to apply multiple concepts. Think like a writer solving craft problems."},{type:"list",items:["12 scenario-based questions","Each tests synthesis of multiple chapters","**80% required to pass** and unlock Part 2","Take your time — this is about understanding, not speed"]}]},{id:"boss-quiz",type:"quiz",quizType:"summative",title:"Part 1 Boss Assessment",passingScore:80,questions:[{id:"boss1-q1",type:"scenario",question:'A writer opens their novel: "The town had been quiet for forty years. Then, on a Tuesday morning, every clock stopped at 9:47 AM." Which neuroscience principles make this opening effective?',options:[{id:"a",text:"Sensory simulation and embodied language create immersion",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"Unexpected change triggers attention; the information gap (why 9:47?) creates curiosity",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"Theory of mind lets us empathize with the townspeople's fear",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"Causal chains establish the narrative logic of the world",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Exactly! The clocks stopping violates our model of reality (unexpected change), and the specific time creates an information gap we desperately want filled. Classic curiosity double-punch.",incorrect:`Think about Chapter 1. What triggers attention? What creates the "itch" of curiosity? This opening works because it **violates expectations** (clocks don't just stop) and creates a **gap** (why that specific time?).`},concepts:["unexpected-change","info-gap-theory","curiosity-gap"]},{id:"boss1-q2",type:"scenario",question:'A reader reports feeling "completely transported" by a novel, losing track of time for hours. According to the neuroscience of Part 1, what is actually happening in their brain?',options:[{id:"a",text:"The reader's salience network has shut down, allowing pure escapism",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"The brain is constructing a neural model of the story world that temporarily replaces the model of physical reality",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"Dopamine floods the pleasure centers, creating addictive reading behavior",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"The reader's theory of mind has fully merged with the protagonist's",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:`Yes! The brain is always running a model of reality — we never experience "raw" reality. When a story is immersive, the brain's model-making machinery constructs the fictional world so convincingly that it displaces the physical one.`,incorrect:`Remember Chapter 2: we experience "hallucinated reality" — our brain's model, not raw reality. Immersion happens when the story triggers the same **model-making machinery** that constructs our experience of the real world.`},concepts:["neural-models","hallucinated-reality","sensory-simulation"]},{id:"boss1-q3",type:"scenario",question:`Two descriptions of a kitchen: (A) "The kitchen was old and messy." (B) "Three days of dishes crusted the sink. A Post-it note on the fridge read 'Call Mom' in handwriting she no longer recognized as her own." Why is B more effective?`,options:[{id:"a",text:"B uses more words, giving the reader more information",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"B uses specific details that build epistemic trust AND reveal character psychology through her perception",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"B creates an information gap by mentioning the mother",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"B triggers the reader's salience network with the number three",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Spot on! The specific details (three days, Post-it, handwriting) build trust that this writer knows this world. But the detail is filtered through the character — she notices the note, and her reaction to her own handwriting reveals something has changed in her. World-building and character-building in one move.",incorrect:'Think about Chapters 3 and 5. Specific details (not just "more words") build **epistemic trust**. And filtering the world through **character perception** makes details do double duty — we learn about the kitchen AND the character simultaneously.'},concepts:["specific-detail","epistemic-trust","world-through-character"]},{id:"boss1-q4",type:"scenario",question:"A villain monologues about their evil plan. The reader finds it boring. A different villain's motivations emerge through small actions and overheard fragments. The reader is fascinated. What explains this difference?",options:[{id:"a",text:"The first villain uses too many metaphors, breaking sensory simulation",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"The second approach engages theory of mind — we're actively modeling the villain's psychology from incomplete information",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"The second villain has a stronger causal chain in their backstory",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"The first approach lacks unexpected change",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Exactly! When motivation is stated outright, our theory of mind has nothing to do. When motivation must be inferred from fragments, we're actively **modeling the character's mind** — which is deeply engaging because it exercises the same neural machinery we use to navigate real social situations.",incorrect:"Remember Chapter 4: we're wired for **theory of mind** — the compulsive modeling of others' mental states. When a character's psychology is delivered as a lecture, there's no modeling to do. When we must piece it together, our social intelligence is fully engaged."},concepts:["theory-of-mind","empathy-engine","character-motivation"]},{id:"boss1-q5",type:"scenario",question:"A writer includes a detailed description of an antique music box in Chapter 2. By the end of the novel, the music box has never appeared again. How does this violate storytelling principles?",options:[{id:"a",text:"It breaks the information gap by providing too much closure",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"It violates Chekhov's Gun — salient details create expectations that must be fulfilled",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"It undermines the character's neural model of reality",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"It disrupts the causal chain by introducing random elements",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Yes! Detailed description flags an element as **salient** — important. The reader's brain stores it as a Chekhov's Gun, expecting payoff. When it never fires, the reader feels betrayed. Every detail should earn its place.",incorrect:"When a writer draws attention to something (makes it **salient**), the reader's brain flags it as important and anticipates it will matter. This is **Chekhov's Gun**. Unfired guns don't just feel irrelevant — they feel like broken promises."},concepts:["salience-network","chekhov-gun","foreshadowing","detail-selection"]},{id:"boss1-q6",type:"scenario",question:'A writer describes heartbreak as "a hollow ache, as if someone had scooped out her chest and left the cold air rushing in." Why does this work better than "She felt very sad"?',options:[{id:"a",text:"It uses more specific details, building epistemic trust",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"It creates an information gap about who hurt her",correct:!1},{id:"c",text:"It employs embodied, sensory metaphor that activates the same neural circuits as physical sensation",correct:!0},{id:"d",text:"It uses unexpected change to signal something important",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:`Exactly! "Hollow," "scooped," "cold air" — these aren't abstractions, they're physical sensations. When we read them, our brain activates some of the same circuits that would fire during actual physical experience. We don't just understand her sadness — we **feel** an echo of it.`,incorrect:"Chapter 6 revealed that metaphors aren't decoration — they're how we think. This description maps abstract emotion onto **physical sensation** (hollow, scooped, cold). The brain processes it through sensorimotor circuits, creating visceral resonance."},concepts:["metaphor-cognition","embodied-language","sensory-metaphor"]},{id:"boss1-q7",type:"scenario",question:'"The queen died. The king died." vs. "The queen died. The king died of grief." According to storytelling neuroscience, why does the second version feel more satisfying?',options:[{id:"a",text:"It creates an information gap about the nature of their relationship",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"The second uses metaphor to make the death more vivid",correct:!1},{id:"c",text:`The "because" (of grief) satisfies the brain's deep need for causal connection`,correct:!0},{id:"d",text:"It builds epistemic trust through specific medical detail",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:'Exactly! The brain craves causation. "Of grief" transforms a chronicle (this happened, then this happened) into a story (this happened BECAUSE of that). The "because principle" shows that humans are more accepting of anything that comes with a reason attached.',incorrect:'Remember Chapter 7: the brain is obsessed with causation. A sequence of events (chronicle) becomes a story when events are linked by **cause and effect**. "Of grief" provides the "because" the brain desperately wants.'},concepts:["causal-chains","because-principle","narrative-logic"]},{id:"boss1-q8",type:"scenario",question:"A character wins the lottery, quits their job, travels the world, but ends the story as essentially the same person. Why does this feel unsatisfying despite all the events?",options:[{id:"a",text:"There's no unexpected change to trigger curiosity",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"The events are surface changes, not identity-level transformation — the character's core self-model remains unchallenged",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"The story lacks sensory metaphors to create immersion",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"There's no clear causal chain between lottery and travel",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Bingo! Events happened TO this character, but nothing happened INSIDE them. Their fundamental self-narrative — who they believe themselves to be — was never challenged. Without identity-level change, we have a travelogue, not a story.",incorrect:"Chapter 8 distinguishes levels of change: surface, behavioral, belief, identity. Winning the lottery and traveling are **surface** changes. The deepest stories require **identity** change — transformation of the character's fundamental self-narrative."},concepts:["meaningful-change","ignition-point","character-driven-plot"]},{id:"boss1-q9",type:"scenario",question:'A writer wants to create a compelling opening. They write: "It was a normal day." Based on Part 1, what fundamental principle does this violate?',options:[{id:"a",text:"It lacks embodied metaphor",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"It confirms the reader's model instead of violating it — no unexpected change, no curiosity trigger",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"It doesn't establish the character's theory of mind",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"It breaks the causal chain before it begins",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:`Exactly! "Normal day" tells the reader's brain: nothing to see here. The prediction matches reality. There's no violation of the model, no unexpected change, no information gap. Curiosity stays dormant. Stories begin when the normal breaks.`,incorrect:`Return to Chapter 1's core insight: our brains are prediction machines. When predictions are confirmed ("normal day"), we ignore. When predictions are violated (unexpected change), we snap to attention. This opening triggers nothing.`},concepts:["unexpected-change","info-gap-theory","curiosity-gap"]},{id:"boss1-q10",type:"scenario",question:"You're crafting the opening of a novel. Based on everything in Part 1, which approach best applies the neuroscience of storytelling?",options:[{id:"a",text:"Open with beautiful description of the setting to immerse the reader in the world",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"Begin with detailed backstory so the reader understands the character fully before events begin",correct:!1},{id:"c",text:"Start with an unexpected change filtered through a character's specific perception, using sensory language, creating an information gap that demands resolution",correct:!0},{id:"d",text:"Open with a philosophical statement that establishes the theme of the book",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Perfect synthesis! You've combined: **unexpected change** (triggers attention), **character perception** (builds character and world simultaneously), **sensory language** (activates neural models), and **information gap** (sustains curiosity). That's Part 1 in a single opening.",incorrect:"Think about ALL the principles: Unexpected change triggers attention (Ch1). Character perception builds world AND character (Ch3-4). Sensory language creates neural models (Ch2, 6). Information gaps sustain curiosity (Ch1). Which option combines them?"},concepts:["unexpected-change","world-through-character","sensory-simulation","info-gap-theory"]},{id:"boss1-q11",type:"scenario",question:"A story establishes that a character is terrified of heights. Later, she casually climbs a tower without hesitation or explanation. What two principles has the author violated?",options:[{id:"a",text:"Curiosity gap and salience — the fear was never paid off",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"Model defense and narrative logic — the reader's model of the character is violated, breaking causal consistency",correct:!0},{id:"c",text:"Epistemic trust and specific detail — the tower wasn't described vividly enough",correct:!1},{id:"d",text:"Theory of mind and empathy — we can't understand her motivation",correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:"Yes! The reader has built a neural model of this character (Ch2), including her fear. When she acts inconsistently without explanation, both model defense (the reader notices the contradiction) and narrative logic (Ch7) are violated.",incorrect:'Two principles are violated: The reader constructed a model of this character (Ch2) that includes the fear. When she acts against it without explanation, model defense flags the inconsistency. Simultaneously, narrative logic (Ch7) breaks — we need a "because" for changed behavior.'},concepts:["model-defense","narrative-logic","neural-models"]},{id:"boss1-q12",type:"scenario",question:'A master storyteller opens a novel: "The morning my mother returned from the dead, I was making toast." What Part 1 principles does this single sentence activate?',options:[{id:"a",text:"Only curiosity gap — we want to know how the mother returned",correct:!1},{id:"b",text:"Specific detail (toast) and epistemic trust create immersion",correct:!1},{id:"c",text:"Curiosity gap (impossible event), neural model disruption (reality violated), theory of mind (narrator's feelings?), and the promise of meaningful change (life can't stay normal)",correct:!0},{id:"d",text:'Causal chain and the "because principle" explain the resurrection',correct:!1}],feedback:{correct:`Perfect synthesis! One sentence does tremendous work: It creates a massive curiosity gap, violates our neural model of reality (people don't return from the dead), activates theory of mind (what IS the narrator experiencing?), and promises meaningful change — the mundane "making toast" will be shattered.`,incorrect:`This sentence is a masterclass in Part 1 integration. It creates a curiosity gap (Ch1), violates our model of reality (Ch2), activates theory of mind (Ch4) as we wonder about the narrator's state, and promises meaningful change (Ch8) — the contrast between "returned from the dead" and "making toast" signals transformation ahead.`},concepts:["curiosity-gap","neural-models","theory-of-mind","meaningful-change"]}]},{id:"boss-complete",type:"content",title:"Assessment Complete",showOnPass:!0,content:[{type:"text",value:"🏆 **Congratulations, World Builder!** You've demonstrated mastery of the foundational principles that make stories work."},{type:"text",value:"You understand that storytelling isn't just craft — it's applied neuroscience. You've earned the **World Builder** badge and 500 XP!"},{type:"callout",variant:"next",value:"**Unlocked:** Part 2 — The Flawed Self. You'll discover how character psychology drives story, and why the most compelling characters are built from sacred flaws."}]},{id:"boss-failed",type:"content",title:"Not Quite There Yet",showOnFail:!0,content:[{type:"text",value:"You need 80% to pass, but don't be discouraged — these are synthesis questions that require deep integration of concepts."},{type:"text",value:"Review the chapters where you struggled, paying special attention to how concepts **connect across chapters**. The best storytellers see patterns, not isolated techniques."},{type:"callout",variant:"info",value:"Take your time reviewing, then return to try again. You've got this!"}]}]};export{e as bossAssessment1};
